      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\( -2 \\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to ensure that there exists some \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = -2 \\). This means we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c = -2 \\]\n\nRearranging this equation, we get:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in the standard form \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). For \\( x \\) to have real solutions, the discriminant of this quadratic equation must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac \\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8 \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 1 - 4c \\]\n\nFor \\( x \\) to have real solutions, the discriminant must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n\\[ 1 - 4c \\geq 0 \\]\n\nSolving this inequality for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[ 1 \\geq 4c \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\geq c \\]\n\\[ c \\leq \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\( -2 \\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}} \\]",
