      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to determine when \\( f(-2) = -2 \\). This is because if \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) \\), there must exist some \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = -2 \\).\n\nFirst, we substitute \\( x = -2 \\) into the function \\( f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nf(-2) = (-2)^2 + 3(-2) + c = 4 - 6 + c = -2 + c\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\( f(-2) = -2 \\), so we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n-2 + c = -2\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\nc = -2 + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo ensure that \\( c = 0 \\) is the largest possible value, we should verify that for \\( c = 0 \\), \\(-2\\) is indeed in the range of \\( f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nf(x) = x^2 + 3x\n\\]\n\nWe need to check if \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) \\). For \\( c = 0 \\), \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x \\) has its minimum value at \\( x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\) (using the vertex formula for a parabola \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\)):\n\n\\[\nf\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) = \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2 + 3\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) = \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{18}{4} = -\\frac{9}{4} = -2.25\n\\]\n\nSince \\( -2 \\) is greater than \\(-2.25\\), it is indeed in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x \\). Therefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
